div></div><div class=3D"_9ecc93a"></div><div class=3D"ds-markdown" style=3D=
"--ds-md-zoom: 1.143;"><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>We are goin=
g to use a pre-trained model (e.g., ResNet) to extract features from the im=
ages.</span><br><span>Then, we will use FAISS to build an index of the feat=
ures from the normal images.</span><br><span>For each test image, we extrac=
t features and compute the distance to the nearest neighbors in the index.<=
/span><br><span>The anomaly score can be the reconstruction error or the di=
stance to the nearest neighbor (or average of k-nearest).</span><br><span>W=
e will then create a heatmap by projecting the error in the feature space b=
ack to the image space (using a method like nearest neighbor search in the =
feature space and then computing the error per patch?).</span></p><p class=
=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>However, note that FAISS is for efficient =
similarity search, and for anomaly heatmaps, we often need a method that ca=
n localize anomalies.</span><br><span>One common approach is to use a metho=
d similar to PaDiM or PatchCore, which use a pre-trained CNN and then build=
 a database of patch features for normal images.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-m=
arkdown-paragraph"><span>Since the requirement is to use FAISS, we can do t=
he following:</span></p><ol start=3D"1"><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragra=
ph"><span>Use a pre-trained CNN (like ResNet) and extract features from an =
intermediate layer that preserves spatial information.</span></p></li><li><=
p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>For each normal image, we extract f=
eature vectors for each patch (spatial location) and then build a FAISS ind=
ex for all the patch features from all normal images.</span></p></li><li><p=
 class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>For a test image, we extract patch f=
eatures and for each patch, we compute the distance to the nearest neighbor=
 in the index. This distance map can be upsampled to the image size to crea=
te a heatmap.</span></p></li></ol><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>=
Steps:</span></p><ul><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>Load pre-=
trained model (e.g., ResNet18) and use the features from the last convoluti=
onal layer.</span></p></li><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>Pre=
process images (resize to 256x256, then center crop to 224x224, and normali=
ze with ImageNet stats).</span></p></li><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragra=
ph"><span>Extract features for normal images and build a FAISS index (using=
 L2 distance or cosine similarity? We'll use L2 for simplicity).</span></p>=
</li></ul><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>However, note that the r=
equirement is to output an anomaly heatmap and an anomaly score for the ima=
ge.</span></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>We can do:</span></p=
><ul><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>Anomaly score: the maximu=
m patch distance in the image (or average of top k patch distances) or the =
average of all patch distances.</span></p></li><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-=
paragraph"><span>Heatmap: the patch distances resized to the image size.</s=
pan></p></li></ul><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>But note: the pa=
tch features are from a convolutional layer, so the spatial resolution is r=
educed. We can interpolate the distance map to the original image size.</sp=
an></p><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>Steps in code:</span></p><o=
l start=3D"1"><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown-paragraph"><span>Load model and r=
emove the fully connected layer.</span></p></li><li><p class=3D"ds-markdown=
